<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Galicia and Lodomeria</h1><p class="st-meta">1772 CE – 1918 CE · capital Lviv</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Habsburg crown land carved from partitioned Poland, a poor but politically lively province that shaped modern Polish and Ukrainian identity.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy</a> took the lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> and south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>. It justified the annexation through old Hungarian claims to the medieval principalities of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-galicia">Halych</a> and Volodymyr, hence the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodomeria">Lodomeria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a> was added in 1846.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a>, called Lemberg, was the capital. After 1867 the province gained wide autonomy within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>, run by Polish nobles through a Diet, with Polish schools and universities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a> and Lviv. It was a land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> and a large Jewish population, known for rural poverty and mass emigration, but also for oil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boryslav">Boryslav</a> and a flourishing Ukrainian national movement.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> was a major battlefield in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. When the empire collapsed in 1918, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-ukrainian-peoples-republic">West Ukrainian People's Republic</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic</a> won the war that followed, taking all of Galicia by 1919.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Galicia and Lodomeria" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: EJavanainen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Galicia-Lodomeria_(1890%E2%80%931918).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1772 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lviv</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Joseph II, Franz Joseph I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic and Greek Catholic Christianity, Judaism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish, Ukrainian, German, Yiddish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>, <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Galicia, Austrian Galicia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1845&amp;focus=kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria" aria-label="Kingdom of Galicia and Lodomeria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5231 443 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Galicia and Lodomeria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5551 699L5457 692M5551 699L5457 693M5551 699L5381 747M5551 699L5381 747M5551 699L5380 747"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5457 692h0M5457 693h0M5381 747h0M5381 747h0M5380 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5551 699h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5551 699h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lviv. <a href="/atlas?year=1845&amp;focus=kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
